# Gradle Project Structure, Wrapper, Build Configuration, Dependencies, and JUnit

## 1. Basic Gradle Project Structure

A typical Java Gradle project can have the following structure:

```text
my-project/
│
├── build.gradle
├── settings.gradle
├── gradlew
├── gradlew.bat
│
├── gradle/
│   └── wrapper/
│       ├── gradle-wrapper.jar
│       └── gradle-wrapper.properties
│
└── src/
    ├── main/
    │   ├── java/
    │   │   └── com/
    │   │       └── example/
    │   │           └── Application.java
    │   │
    │   └── resources/
    │       └── application.properties
    │
    └── test/
        ├── java/
        │   └── com/
        │       └── example/
        │           └── ApplicationTest.java
        │
        └── resources/
```

### Important files

| File/Directory                             | Purpose                                                 |
| ------------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------- |
| `build.gradle`                             | Build configuration, plugins, dependencies, tasks, etc. |
| `settings.gradle`                          | Project name and multi-module project configuration     |
| `gradlew`                                  | Gradle Wrapper script for Linux/macOS                   |
| `gradlew.bat`                              | Gradle Wrapper script for Windows                       |
| `gradle/wrapper/gradle-wrapper.jar`        | Implements Gradle Wrapper logic                         |
| `gradle/wrapper/gradle-wrapper.properties` | Specifies the Gradle version/distribution               |
| `src/main/java`                            | Main Java source code                                   |
| `src/main/resources`                       | Application resources/configuration                     |
| `src/test/java`                            | Test source code                                        |
| `src/test/resources`                       | Test resources                                          |

---

# 2. Gradle Wrapper

The Gradle Wrapper allows a project to use a specific Gradle version without requiring that exact version to be installed globally on the machine.

The important files are:

```text
gradlew
gradlew.bat
gradle/
└── wrapper/
    ├── gradle-wrapper.jar
    └── gradle-wrapper.properties
```

### Why is the Wrapper useful?

Suppose:

```text
Project requires Gradle 8.10
Your machine has Gradle 7.6
```

If you run:

```text
gradle build
```

you may encounter compatibility problems because the globally installed Gradle version is being used.

With the Wrapper:

```text
gradlew build
```

the project uses the Gradle version configured by the project.

Conceptually:

```text
gradlew build
      │
      ▼
Reads Gradle Wrapper configuration
      │
      ▼
Uses project's configured Gradle version
      │
      ▼
Runs the build
```

This makes builds more consistent across developers and CI/CD environments.

---

# 3. `gradlew` and `gradlew.bat`

There are two Wrapper scripts:

```text
gradlew
gradlew.bat
```

### Linux/macOS

Use:

```bash
./gradlew build
```

### Windows

Use:

```cmd
gradlew.bat build
```

or commonly:

```cmd
gradlew build
```

The scripts ultimately use:

```text
gradle-wrapper.jar
```

to start the appropriate Gradle version.

---

# 4. `gradle-wrapper.properties`

The file:

```text
gradle/wrapper/gradle-wrapper.properties
```

contains the configuration used by the Gradle Wrapper.

Example:

```properties
distributionBase=GRADLE_USER_HOME
distributionPath=wrapper/dists
distributionUrl=https\://services.gradle.org/distributions/gradle-8.5-bin.zip
zipStoreBase=GRADLE_USER_HOME
zipStorePath=wrapper/dists
```

## 4.1 `distributionBase`

```properties
distributionBase=GRADLE_USER_HOME
```

`GRADLE_USER_HOME` represents the user's Gradle home directory.

On Windows, it is typically:

```text
C:\Users\<username>\.gradle
```

Therefore, Gradle uses:

```text
C:\Users\<username>\.gradle
```

as the base location.

---

## 4.2 `distributionPath`

```properties
distributionPath=wrapper/dists
```

This specifies where the downloaded Gradle distribution is stored inside `GRADLE_USER_HOME`.

Conceptually:

```text
C:\Users\<username>\.gradle\
└── wrapper\
    └── dists\
```

---

## 4.3 `distributionUrl`

```properties
distributionUrl=https\://services.gradle.org/distributions/gradle-8.5-bin.zip
```

This is the most important property.

It tells the Wrapper which Gradle distribution to use.

In this example:

```text
Gradle version = 8.5
Distribution = gradle-8.5-bin.zip
```

When you run:

```text
gradlew build
```

the Wrapper checks whether the required Gradle distribution is already available.

If it is not available, it downloads:

```text
gradle-8.5-bin.zip
```

and uses it to run the build.

---

## 4.4 `zipStoreBase`

```properties
zipStoreBase=GRADLE_USER_HOME
```

This specifies the base directory where the downloaded Gradle ZIP is stored.

Typically:

```text
C:\Users\<username>\.gradle
```

---

## 4.5 `zipStorePath`

```properties
zipStorePath=wrapper/dists
```

This specifies the path inside `GRADLE_USER_HOME`.

Conceptually:

```text
C:\Users\<username>\.gradle\
└── wrapper\
    └── dists\
```

The Wrapper may create additional generated directories underneath this location.

---

# 5. `gradle-wrapper.jar`

The file:

```text
gradle/wrapper/gradle-wrapper.jar
```

is a small Java program that implements the Gradle Wrapper logic.

Think of the process as:

```text
gradlew / gradlew.bat
          │
          ▼
gradle-wrapper.jar
          │
          ▼
reads gradle-wrapper.properties
          │
          ▼
finds required Gradle version
          │
          ▼
downloads Gradle if necessary
          │
          ▼
runs Gradle
          │
          ▼
builds the project
```

### Why is the JAR required?

Suppose the project specifies:

```properties
distributionUrl=https\://services.gradle.org/distributions/gradle-8.10-bin.zip
```

When you execute:

```text
gradlew build
```

the Wrapper needs some code to:

1. Read `gradle-wrapper.properties`
2. Determine the required Gradle version
3. Download Gradle if necessary
4. Start that Gradle version
5. Execute the requested task

That logic is contained in `gradle-wrapper.jar`.

You normally **do not modify this JAR manually**.

---

# 6. `gradle-wrapper.jar` vs `gradle-wrapper.properties`

A simple way to remember the difference:

```text
gradle-wrapper.jar
        ↓
Does the Wrapper work

gradle-wrapper.properties
        ↓
Tells the Wrapper which Gradle distribution to use
```

For example:

```text
gradle/
└── wrapper/
    ├── gradle-wrapper.jar
    └── gradle-wrapper.properties
```

Think of it as:

```text
JAR          = HOW
properties   = WHICH
```

The JAR contains the Wrapper implementation, while the properties file specifies the Gradle distribution configuration.

---

# 7. `settings.gradle`

A simple `settings.gradle` file:

```groovy
rootProject.name = 'my-project'
```

This tells Gradle:

```text
The name of my project is my-project.
```

`settings.gradle` is also important for multi-module projects because it can define which subprojects/modules belong to the build.

---

# 8. Basic `build.gradle`

A basic Java Gradle configuration can be:

```groovy
plugins {
    id 'java'
}

group = 'com.example'
version = '1.0.0'

repositories {
    mavenCentral()
}

dependencies {
    implementation 'org.apache.commons:commons-lang3:3.18.0'

    testImplementation 'org.junit.jupiter:junit-jupiter:5.12.2'
}

test {
    useJUnitPlatform()
}
```

---

# 9. Understanding the `java` Plugin

```groovy
plugins {
    id 'java'
}
```

This tells Gradle:

```text
This is a Java project.
```

The Java plugin provides tasks such as:

```text
compileJava
compileTestJava
test
jar
build
clean
```

For example:

```text
gradle build
```

can compile the application, compile tests, run tests, and create the JAR.

---

# 10. `group` and `version`

```groovy
group = 'com.example'
version = '1.0.0'
```

These identify the project.

For example, when Gradle creates a JAR, you may get:

```text
build/libs/my-project-1.0.0.jar
```

The project name comes from:

```groovy
rootProject.name = 'my-project'
```

and the version comes from:

```groovy
version = '1.0.0'
```

---

# 11. `repositories`

```groovy
repositories {
    mavenCentral()
}
```

This tells Gradle where it should search for dependencies.

For example:

```groovy
implementation 'org.apache.commons:commons-lang3:3.18.0'
```

Gradle searches the configured repositories for this library.

In this example:

```text
build.gradle
      │
      ▼
repositories
      │
      ▼
Maven Central
      │
      ▼
Find commons-lang3
```

---

# 12. Dependencies

Dependencies tell Gradle which external libraries the project requires.

Example:

```groovy
dependencies {
    implementation 'org.apache.commons:commons-lang3:3.18.0'

    testImplementation 'org.junit.jupiter:junit-jupiter:5.12.2'
}
```

There are different dependency configurations.

## `implementation`

```groovy
implementation 'org.apache.commons:commons-lang3:3.18.0'
```

This dependency is required by the main application.

For example:

```java
import org.apache.commons.lang3.StringUtils;
```

The `StringUtils` class comes from:

```text
commons-lang3
```

The flow is:

```text
build.gradle
     │
     ▼
implementation dependency
     │
     ▼
Gradle downloads commons-lang3
     │
     ▼
Java compiler gets the library
     │
     ▼
App.java can use StringUtils
```

---

# 13. `testImplementation`

```groovy
testImplementation 'org.junit.jupiter:junit-jupiter:5.12.2'
```

This dependency is intended for tests.

The difference is:

```text
implementation
      ↓
Main application

testImplementation
      ↓
Test code
```

For example:

```java
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
```

comes from JUnit.

---

# 14. Example Java Application

Create:

```text
src/main/java/com/example/App.java
```

Example:

```java
package com.example;

import org.apache.commons.lang3.StringUtils;

public class App {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        String name = "Gradle";

        System.out.println(
            StringUtils.isNotBlank(name)
        );
    }
}
```

The important part is:

```java
import org.apache.commons.lang3.StringUtils;
```

This class is available because of:

```groovy
implementation 'org.apache.commons:commons-lang3:3.18.0'
```

---

# 15. Example Test

Create:

```text
src/test/java/com/example/AppTest.java
```

Example:

```java
package com.example;

import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;

import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertTrue;

class AppTest {

    @Test
    void testName() {
        assertTrue(true);
    }
}
```

JUnit comes from the test dependencies.

---

# 16. `useJUnitPlatform()`

In `build.gradle`:

```groovy
test {
    useJUnitPlatform()
}
```

This tells Gradle to use the JUnit Platform when discovering and executing tests.

The JUnit Platform is used by JUnit 5 for test discovery and execution.

---

# 17. Build the Project

From the project directory:

```text
gradle build
```

or, preferably when the Wrapper is available:

```text
gradlew build
```

The process is approximately:

```text
gradlew build
      │
      ▼
Read settings.gradle
      │
      ▼
Read build.gradle
      │
      ▼
Apply Java plugin
      │
      ▼
Resolve dependencies
      │
      ▼
Compile Java
      │
      ▼
Compile tests
      │
      ▼
Run tests
      │
      ▼
Create JAR
```

After a successful build:

```text
my-project/
└── build/
    ├── classes/
    ├── test-results/
    ├── reports/
    ├── libs/
    │   └── my-project-1.0.0.jar
    └── ...
```

---

# 18. Run Tests Separately

You can run only the tests using:

```text
gradlew test
```

Gradle then executes the test configuration:

```groovy
test {
    useJUnitPlatform()
}
```

and discovers and executes the JUnit tests.

---

# 19. Complete Gradle Build Picture

The overall relationship can be understood as:

```text
                         my-project
                             │
              ┌──────────────┴──────────────┐
              ▼                             ▼
        settings.gradle                build.gradle
              │                             │
              │                  ┌──────────┼───────────┐
              │                  ▼          ▼           ▼
              │               Plugins   Dependencies  Test config
              │                  │          │           │
              │                  ▼          ▼           ▼
              │              Java plugin  Libraries  JUnit Platform
              │                  │          │           │
              └──────────────────┴──────────┴───────────┘
                                         │
                                         ▼
                                   gradlew build
                                         │
                              ┌──────────┴──────────┐
                              ▼                     ▼
                         Tests pass             JAR created
                                                    │
                                                    ▼
                                            build/libs/
```

---

# 20. Complete Example Project

The complete project can look like this:

```text
my-project/
│
├── build.gradle
├── settings.gradle
├── gradlew
├── gradlew.bat
│
├── gradle/
│   └── wrapper/
│       ├── gradle-wrapper.jar
│       └── gradle-wrapper.properties
│
└── src/
    ├── main/
    │   ├── java/
    │   │   └── com/
    │   │       └── example/
    │   │           └── App.java
    │   │
    │   └── resources/
    │
    └── test/
        ├── java/
        │   └── com/
        │       └── example/
        │           └── AppTest.java
        │
        └── resources/
```

---

# 21. JUnit Dependency Management with BOM

A more flexible JUnit configuration is:

```groovy
dependencies {

    testImplementation platform('org.junit:junit-bom:5.12.2')

    testImplementation 'org.junit.jupiter:junit-jupiter'

    testRuntimeOnly 'org.junit.platform:junit-platform-launcher:1.12.2'

    implementation 'org.apache.commons:commons-lang3:3.18.0'
}
```

This configuration can work because the JUnit BOM manages compatible versions of the JUnit modules.

## What is the BOM?

BOM means:

```text
Bill of Materials
```

The JUnit BOM:

```groovy
testImplementation platform('org.junit:junit-bom:5.12.2')
```

provides version information for related JUnit dependencies.

Therefore, you can write:

```groovy
testImplementation 'org.junit.jupiter:junit-jupiter'
```

without explicitly specifying its version.

The version is obtained from the BOM.

---

# 22. Why `junit-platform-launcher` Is Added

This dependency:

```groovy
testRuntimeOnly 'org.junit.platform:junit-platform-launcher:1.12.2'
```

provides the JUnit Platform Launcher at test runtime.

The important distinction is:

```text
testImplementation
        ↓
Required to compile test code

testRuntimeOnly
        ↓
Required when tests are executed
```

So:

```groovy
testImplementation 'org.junit.jupiter:junit-jupiter'
```

is used for compiling the test code, while:

```groovy
testRuntimeOnly 'org.junit.platform:junit-platform-launcher:1.12.2'
```

makes the launcher available at test runtime.

---

# 23. Attempt to Force JUnit Platform Version

Another approach is:

```groovy
configurations.all {
    resolutionStrategy.eachDependency { dependency ->
        if (dependency.requested.group == 'org.junit.platform') {
            dependency.useVersion '1.12.2'
        }
    }
}
```

This approach is different from using a BOM.

It tells Gradle:

```text
For dependencies whose group is:
org.junit.platform

force the requested version to:
1.12.2
```

Conceptually:

```text
Dependency resolution
        │
        ▼
Check each dependency
        │
        ▼
Is group == org.junit.platform?
        │
       Yes
        │
        ▼
Use version 1.12.2
```

However, this is **not equivalent to the JUnit BOM**.

The BOM manages a coordinated set of JUnit versions, while `resolutionStrategy` modifies dependency resolution based on a rule.

Therefore, if the BOM-based configuration works while the `resolutionStrategy` configuration does not, the BOM approach is generally preferable for managing JUnit's related module versions.

---

# 24. BOM vs `resolutionStrategy`

### BOM approach

```groovy
dependencies {
    testImplementation platform('org.junit:junit-bom:5.12.2')

    testImplementation 'org.junit.jupiter:junit-jupiter'

    testRuntimeOnly 'org.junit.platform:junit-platform-launcher:1.12.2'
}
```

Conceptually:

```text
JUnit BOM
   │
   ├── JUnit Jupiter
   ├── JUnit Platform
   └── Other compatible JUnit modules
```

The BOM is designed to keep related JUnit components compatible.

### Resolution strategy approach

```groovy
configurations.all {
    resolutionStrategy.eachDependency { dependency ->
        if (dependency.requested.group == 'org.junit.platform') {
            dependency.useVersion '1.12.2'
        }
    }
}
```

This is a general Gradle dependency-resolution mechanism.

It does not provide the same coordinated dependency management that the JUnit BOM provides.

---

# 25. Recommended JUnit Configuration

For the example project, a clean configuration is:

```groovy
plugins {
    id 'java'
}

group = 'com.example'
version = '1.0.0'

repositories {
    mavenCentral()
}

dependencies {

    implementation 'org.apache.commons:commons-lang3:3.18.0'

    testImplementation platform('org.junit:junit-bom:5.12.2')
    testImplementation 'org.junit.jupiter:junit-jupiter'

    testRuntimeOnly 'org.junit.platform:junit-platform-launcher:1.12.2'
}

test {
    useJUnitPlatform()
}
```

The main concepts are:

```text
Gradle Wrapper
    ↓
Controls Gradle version

settings.gradle
    ↓
Defines project structure/name

build.gradle
    ↓
Defines how the project is built

plugins
    ↓
Adds Gradle capabilities

repositories
    ↓
Defines where dependencies are found

dependencies
    ↓
Defines libraries required by the project

BOM
    ↓
Manages compatible dependency versions

testImplementation
    ↓
Dependencies required to compile tests

testRuntimeOnly
    ↓
Dependencies required when tests execute

useJUnitPlatform()
    ↓
Uses JUnit Platform for test execution

gradlew build
    ↓
Compiles + tests + packages the application
```
